# encode_rd — the full matched-wall-clock encode RD sweep

harness: scripts/encode_rd/{run_grid.py, run_full_sweep.sh, analyze_matched.py,
         bdrate_matched.py, bytes_model.py, payload_identity.py,
         merge_and_analyze.sh} + examples/rd_tool.rs
         + zenavif-serialize/examples/container_cost.rs
methodology: scripts/encode_rd/README.md — read it before quoting a number
commit: SEE THE TSV HEADER (each tier records its own)
date: 2026-08-08

## READ THIS FIRST — what is NOT covered

1. **4096 px is not covered for photo or line-art, and 2048 px is not in the RD
   grid at all.** No local photo source exceeds 2048 px (clic2025 is natively
   2048) and no local line-art source exceeds 796 px (`gb82-sc/graph.png`; the
   CID22 charts are 512). `rd_tool prep` refuses to upscale — fabricated
   high-frequency detail is not something an encoder should be judged on — so
   the tier simply does not exist on this box. The RD grid is 64 / 256 / 1024.
   The size model reaches 2048 (photo + screen) and 4096 (screen only, from
   `qoi-benchmark/screenshot_web`, the only local sources over 2940 px), on a
   reduced rate grid.
2. **The `zenrav1e` arm is the rav1e CLI, NOT zenavif's encode path.** Same
   encoder core, different search configuration: zenavif goes through
   zenravif's `SpeedTweaks`, which pins `complex_prediction_modes=false` and
   `encode_bottomup=false`, caps `non_square_partition_max` at 8 for speed>=4,
   collapses `partition_range` to (16,16) at speed>=9, gates CDEF/LRF on the
   quantizer, and maps quality->quantizer through its own curve
   (`src/encode_plan.rs`). Percent-level RD differences are expected. Plane 2
   (PNG -> AVIF through zenavif / cavif / avifenc) is still not wired.
3. **12 images: 5 photo, 4 screen, 3 line-art.** That is n=5/4/3 per class in
   every aggregate. The sweep discipline asks for 50 per class for percentile
   fits; that is a fleet job, not a laptop job. Image count was the axis cut,
   per the instruction to reduce images before reducing q or size resolution —
   the 20-point rate grid and the size tiers are intact.
4. **zenrav1e speeds 0-5 are not in the RD grid at 1024**, and 0-2 not at 256.
   s3 costs 6.8 s per encode at 1024, i.e. 2.1 h per image at full rate
   density. Consequence: at 1024 the zenrav1e frontier ends at ~1.1 s and the
   analysis correctly refuses to compare it above that. The full ladder IS
   measured in `encode_rd_probe_ladder_2026-08-08.tsv` at 2 reps.
5. **8-bit, 4:2:0, 1 thread, 1 tile, CQP only.** No 10-bit, no alpha, no
   animation, no thread-count axis, no tiling axis. Tiling changes the
   bitstream, so folding threads into the time axis would trade RD for speed
   silently.
6. **Not niced, but the box was not empty.** Median 0.37 of 12 cores busy with
   foreign work during timed samples (p90 0.65, p99 1.46, max 4.33); the
   free-core gate never had to wait. Two builds ran on the box: `rd_tool`
   before the sweep started, and a 7-second `container_cost` build during rep 2
   of the 64 px tier. Both niced. Five rotated reps and a median absorb it, but
   it is on the record rather than claimed away.

## Host and configuration

host: Apple M4 Pro, 8 performance + 4 efficiency cores, 24 GB, macOS 26.5.2
scheduling gate: every tier verified NOT to be in the darwin background QoS
    class before measuring (calibration loop 5.7-6.0x slower when explicitly
    backgrounded). See scripts/encode_rd/README.md section 3 — on macOS `nice`
    costs 4% and background QoS costs 5.9x, and they are different mechanisms.
time  = full process wall clock, never niced, median of 5 rotated reps.
bytes = bytes_av1, the AV1 payload with the IVF container stripped (44 B for a
        still). Two arms write IVF and one writes bare OBU, so comparing file
        size would hand the OBU arm 44 free bytes.
config: 8-bit 4:2:0, one key frame, still-picture, 1 thread, 1 tile, CQP,
        every adaptive-QP / psychovisual layer at each encoder's own default.

## Grid

sizes:  64, 256, 1024 long-edge cap (RD grid); + 2048, 4096 (size model only)
rates:  each arm's own 20-point quantizer grid, spaced for even ACHIEVED
        QUALITY (~7 ssim2 steps from ~92 down to ~15), NOT even knob spacing
reps:   5, interleaved, visit order rotated per rep
images: photo    clic2025/training/{4cd6910a, 7e499613, ddcd24d9, 14ab4af2,
                 1e2f9d41} — picked at evenly spaced quantiles of PNG
                 bytes/pixel (0.189 to 2.363) so the five are not one texture
        screen   gb82-sc/{codec_wiki, gui, terminal},
                 qoi-benchmark/screenshot_web/en.wikipedia.org
        line-art gb82-sc/graph, CID22/{Boxplot, newplot}

ladders — DIFFERENT PER SIZE AND PER ARM, which is the whole point:

    64    zenrav1e 10,9,8,7,6,3,2,1,0   aom 9..0   svtc 9..0   svtrs 9..0
    256   zenrav1e 10,9,8,7,6,3         aom 9,8,7,6,5,4,3,2,0  (svtc, svtrs same)
    1024  zenrav1e 10,9,8,7,6           aom 9,8,6,5,4,2
          svtc 9,8,7,6,5,4,3,2,0        svtrs 9,8,7,6,5,4,3

Justification, all measured on the sizing probe:
  * four-way time overlap is 11.8-136 ms at 256 and 103-1252 ms at 1024, so the
    rung set has to move with size or the comparison has nothing to stand on.
  * zenrav1e s4/s5 are STRICTLY DOMINATED by s3 (slower AND larger at both
    sizes) — a dominated rung is never the right choice, so paying full rate
    density for it buys nothing. Kept in the probe as the evidence.
  * svtc/svtrs presets 10-13 are byte-identical to preset 9 and cost the same.
    The SVT still-picture ladder has 10 distinct rungs, not 14.

## Files

encode_rd_sweep_2026-08-08.tsv.zst        the RD grid (64/256/1024)
encode_rd_sweep_fit_2026-08-08.tsv.zst    the size model grid (adds 2048/4096)
encode_rd_sweep_floor_2026-08-08.tsv      the 4:2:0 round-trip ceiling per input
encode_rd_sweep_analysis_2026-08-08.txt.zst   analyze_matched + bdrate + bytes
encode_rd_probe_ladder_2026-08-08.tsv     the full-ladder sizing probe
Encoded bitstreams: content-addressed <sha256>.{ivf,obu}. Every row carries its
enc_sha256, so a scalar score can be rejoined to the exact bitstream forever.

## The caveat that scopes every matched-time number below

At small sizes the wall clock is mostly NOT encoding. Three arms report their
own encode time, so this is measured directly rather than fitted
(`scripts/encode_rd/overhead_share.py`); on the committed validation grid:

    (wall - self)/wall              64px    256px    796px   1024px
      aom      cpu8                90.3%    62.7%    25.1%    17.9%
      aom      cpu4                46.2%     6.8%     1.8%     3.0%
      svtc     p8                  68.1%    73.9%    27.4%    26.2%
      svtc     p4                  53.0%    21.7%     4.0%     2.5%
      zenrav1e s8                  60.1%    10.3%     2.6%     1.2%
      zenrav1e s4                   9.3%     1.1%     0.2%     0.0%

So: **at 64 px a matched-wall-clock ranking of the fast arms is a comparison of
process spawn, not of compression.** The useful reading at 64 px is the RD curve
and the bpp intercept. At 1024 px the clock is 0-3% overhead on the slow rungs
and up to 26% on svtc's fastest, so the ranking there is a real encoder ranking.
(SvtAv1EncApp reports integer milliseconds, so its share is quantised below
~10 ms.)

This is also the honest frame for the whole harness: process wall is what an
image pipeline pays per image, which is why it is the clock — but an in-process
encoder API would rank the fast arms differently at thumbnail sizes.

## How to re-run

    bash scripts/encode_rd/run_full_sweep.sh        # ~5.5 h on this host
    bash scripts/encode_rd/run_topup_1024.sh        # ~35 min, slow-rung frontier
    bash scripts/encode_rd/merge_and_analyze.sh     # merge tiers + every reduction

Progress streams continuously to `$OUT/prog_<tier>.tsv` (one flushed line per
timed encode) and the clocks are checkpointed to `prog_<tier>.ckpt.json` after
every rep, so an interrupted multi-hour tier is not a total loss.

## Results — 64 px tier (9,360 cells, 46,800 encodes, 1,978 s, 0 failed)

Every cell kept all 5 reps; 0 samples dropped for foreign CPU; **0 samples ever
waited** for the free-core gate. (The run summary's "869 s waiting" is the cost
of the `ps -Ao pid,%cpu,comm -r` poll taken before every sample — 18.6 ms x
46,800 — not contention. It sits outside the timed region so it cannot
contaminate a measurement, but it inflates sweep wall cost by ~44% at 64 px,
where the poll costs about what the encode does.)

Timing spread at 64 px: **median 5.36 %, p90 12.67 %, max 226 %** — far worse
than the 1.2-3.7 % the validation grid saw at larger sizes, because jitter is
roughly constant in milliseconds and these encodes are 1-5 ms. **Treat any 64 px
difference under ~13 % as unmeasurable.**

### Ranking at matched wall clock, 64 px

Four-way time overlap 5.3-12.7 ms. Ratio of bytes to the per-point best arm,
geometric mean over the COMMON support (the (image, quality) points where every
arm reaches the budget); 1.000 = on the frontier everywhere.

    line-art  5.3 ms  n=24  aom 1.026  svtc 1.070  svtrs 1.190  zenrav1e 1.423
    line-art 12.7 ms  n=25  aom 1.050  svtc 1.213  svtrs 1.291  zenrav1e 1.409
    photo     5.3 ms  n=30  svtc 1.038  aom 1.087  svtrs 1.135  zenrav1e 1.229
    photo    12.7 ms  n=51  svtc 1.008  aom 1.045  svtrs 1.086  zenrav1e 1.261
    screen    5.3 ms  n=22  svtc 1.058  aom 1.078  svtrs 1.164  zenrav1e 1.501
    screen   12.7 ms  n=25  aom 1.006  svtc 1.081  svtrs 1.126  zenrav1e 1.467

**zenrav1e is last in every class at every budget, by 23-50 %** (and 89 % in the
worst screen row) — an order of magnitude outside the 13 % noise floor. svtrs
(pure-Rust SVT) beats it by a wide margin everywhere. aom and svtc trade first
place and their gap is often inside the noise floor.

Read this with the overhead table above: at 64 px roughly half to nine tenths of
the budget is process spawn, so what the ranking really says is "given ~2-9 ms
of actual encoding, who spends it best".

### The 4:2:0 floor at 64 px is the dominant cost for some content

ssim2 of the unencoded source through the harness colour pair (ceiling for the
product, and why the analysis defaults to `*_floor`):

    codec_wiki   26.9      ddcd24d9 (photo)  27.5      7e499613 (photo)  52.5
    gui          54.9      terminal          57.0      graph (line-art)  71.2
    Boxplot      74.6      newplot           84.9      1e2f9d41 (photo)  89.4
    4cd6910a     90.1      en.wikipedia.org  91.8

On the worst two, **4:2:0 chroma alone costs more quality than any encoder
choice does**. Nothing in this sweep tests 4:4:4 — every arm is pinned to 4:2:0 —
so this is a measurement, not a recommendation, but it is the strongest
follow-up the grid points at.

### BD-rate at matched wall clock, 64 px

Integral over the quality range, not a single slice. Negative = A needs fewer
bytes at equal quality AND equal measured encode time. Median over images.

    A vs B            budget   line-art        photo         screen
    aom vs zenrav1e    5.3 ms  -20.67 (n=3)  -11.58 (n=4)  -21.46 (n=4)
    aom vs zenrav1e    9.5 ms  -39.25 (n=3)  -21.23 (n=5)  -24.72 (n=4)
    aom vs svtrs       5.3 ms  -12.07 (n=3)   -6.24 (n=5)   -7.69 (n=4)
    aom vs svtrs       9.5 ms  -20.54 (n=3)   -3.39 (n=5)   -9.24 (n=4)
    aom vs svtc        5.3 ms   -5.07 (n=3)   +2.43 (n=5)   +3.06 (n=4)
    aom vs svtc        9.5 ms   -9.55 (n=3)   +6.82 (n=4)   -6.15 (n=4)

The ordering is stable and large where it matters: **zenrav1e needs 12-39 % more
bytes than aom for the same quality at the same spend**, and svtrs sits between
them. aom vs svtc is the only pair that changes sign with content — svtc wins on
photo by 2-7 %, aom wins on line-art by 5-10 %.

## Results — 1024 px tier (2,160 cells, 0 failed, all 5 reps kept)

4 images: 4cd6910a + ddcd24d9 (photo), codec_wiki (screen), graph (line-art, its
source caps at 796 px so it is tagged 796). n=2 photo / 1 screen / 1 line-art —
the cut recorded above.

**This is the tier the decision rests on, and the instrument is at its best
here:**

    timing spread   median 1.96 %, p90 5.34 %      (64 px: 5.36 % / 12.67 %)
    overhead share  0.2-1.9 % on the slow rungs    (64 px: 46-90 %)
                    12-36 % only on the fastest rungs
    4-arm overlap   79.8-704.8 ms @1024, 41.1-412.8 ms @796 — a 9x window

### Reach is itself a result

Quality-grid points (of 35) each arm can reach at each budget, median over
images. An arm missing from a budget is not losing — it *cannot spend that long,
or go that fast*:

    @1024        79.8ms  164.9ms  341.0ms  704.8ms
      svtc           35       35       35       33
      svtrs          35       35       35       27
      aom            29       28       28       23
      zenrav1e        6       35       34       15

**zenrav1e is the only arm that cannot populate either end.** Its ladder bottoms
out near 80 ms and its useful top is ~700 ms; svtc spans 8 ms to 2.1 s.

### Ranking at matched wall clock, 1024 px

Bytes relative to the per-point best arm, geometric mean over common support:

    line-art @796   41.1 ms  n=29  svtc 1.000  aom 1.167  svtrs 1.327  [zenrav1e n/r]
    line-art @796   88.7 ms  n=29  svtc 1.000  aom 1.133  svtrs 1.244  zenrav1e 2.342
    photo    @1024 164.9 ms  n=58  svtc 1.005  aom 1.102  svtrs 1.139  zenrav1e 1.460
    photo    @1024 341.0 ms  n=61  svtc 1.005  aom 1.073  svtrs 1.097  zenrav1e 1.282
    photo    @1024 704.8 ms  n=23  svtc 1.018  aom 1.041  svtrs 1.081  zenrav1e 1.252
    screen   @1024  79.8 ms   n=6  svtc 1.000  aom 1.087  svtrs 1.161  zenrav1e 2.913
    screen   @1024 341.0 ms  n=17  svtc 1.001  aom 1.065  svtrs 1.117  zenrav1e 1.457

**svtc is on the frontier at essentially every point (1.000-1.018) in every
class at every budget.** Order is svtc < aom < svtrs < zenrav1e, and it does not
change with content or quality band at this size.

### BD-rate at matched wall clock, 1024 px

    A vs B             budget    line-art@796   photo@1024   screen@1024
    aom vs svtc         ~80 ms      +16.47        +15.60        +9.71
    aom vs svtc        ~340 ms      +11.09         +6.71        +7.44
    aom vs svtc        ~705 ms       +5.76         +6.65        +5.50
    svtc vs svtrs       ~80 ms      -23.95        -13.22       -17.42
    svtc vs svtrs      ~705 ms       -9.42         -6.32       -11.30
    aom vs svtrs        ~80 ms      -12.17         -5.12       -10.72
    aom vs svtrs       ~705 ms       -8.03         -0.74        -2.74
    svtc vs zenrav1e    ~80 ms      -52.72        -37.04       -55.69
    svtc vs zenrav1e   ~340 ms      -37.85        -11.28       -26.22
    aom vs zenrav1e    ~340 ms      -34.32         -5.34       -26.59

**The sign of aom-vs-svtc flips between sizes.** At 64 px aom beat svtc on photo
by 2-7 %; at 1024 px svtc beats aom on photo by 6-16 %. A single-size benchmark
would have got this backwards.

## Results — the size model, 64 to 4096 px (600 cells)

2 full-page web screenshots (the only local sources over 2940 px) at five sizes,
rungs {9,8,6}, 4 rates, 5 reps. Reduced rate/rung density on purpose: this grid
exists for the size model, so what it needs is pixel SPAN, not RD resolution.

CAVEAT: prep caps the LONG edge and these sources are 1313x6097 / 1551x4354, so
the small rows are extreme slivers (10x64, 22x64 at the 64 tier; 42x256, 90x256
at 256). The 1024/2048/4096 rows (168x1024 to 1458x4096) are reasonable shapes.

### ms/MP is not remotely constant — up to a 290x swing for one arm

    arm/rung        64px   256px  1024px  2048px  4096px   swing
    svtc     p9     2336     158      19      11       8    290x
    svtc     p6     2577     310      70      80      64     40x
    aom      cpu6   3288     464     194     214     141     23x
    svtrs    p6     2750     856     420     393     340      8x
    zenrav1e s6     4608    1791    1315    1009     816      6x

**Any single ms/MP figure is wrong at every size but one.** This is the whole
reason the discipline demands the intercept.

### t = alpha + beta*MP, fitted per image across all five sizes

r2 0.991-0.999, zero negative alphas, exponent 0.35-0.82 — every arm strongly
sub-linear, so alpha here absorbs small-size inefficiency and C3's local alpha is
the usable fixed-cost estimate.

    arm/rung      alpha_ms   beta_ms/MP   exponent   local_alpha_ms
    svtc     p9       4.81         10.5      0.352        3.25
    svtc     p6      15.32         94.6      0.606        3.39
    aom      cpu6    41.05        188.2      0.672        4.36
    svtrs    p6      47.96        443.0      0.771        3.12
    zenrav1e s6     211.90       1167.2      0.821        5.25

**beta is the speed story.** At rung 6 the per-megapixel cost is svtc 94.6, aom
188.2 (2.0x), svtrs 443.0 (**4.7x**), zenrav1e 1167.2 (**12.3x**). The
svtrs/svtc slope ratio of 4.7x independently reproduces zenav1-svt's own
3.5-4.9x figure, on real content and through a different harness.

**svtrs's local alpha is the LOWEST of the four** (3.12 ms) despite paying a PNG
decode inside its timed region that the y4m arms do not. So the process-wall
clock is not quietly taxing that arm — its cost is all slope, which is the
honest direction for the comparison.

### The byte axis, and why its straight-line intercept is a trap

At fixed quantizer, bytes are sub-linear too (exponent 0.733-0.779). A single
line over three decades of pixels cannot fit, so least squares dumps the
curvature into the intercept: the naive fit reports alpha = 3.7-5.8 kB, which is
nonsense for an AV1 still and is nearly identical across all four arms — the tell
that it is a curve-shape artifact, not an encoder property. Honest numbers:

    arm/rung        local_alpha_B   min_payload_B
    svtc     p6           147.6            68
    svtrs    p6           147.6            68
    aom      cpu6         158.7            67
    zenrav1e s6           171.9            77

The minimum observed payload is assumption-free: a whole still bitstream, headers
included, fits in 62-79 bytes, so the AV1 fixed part cannot exceed that.

**Which makes the shipped-file arithmetic surprising: the AV1 bitstream's own
fixed cost is under ~70 bytes, while zenavif's ISOBMFF container is a flat 242.
For a thumbnail the container costs roughly 3.5x what the codec's headers do.**

